  "ResearchDossier": {
    "topic": "why it's called Boxing Day",
    "summary": "Boxing Day's name is linked to Christmas boxes: in Britain tradesmen collected 'Christmas boxes' of money or presents after Christmas as thanks for good service (Pepys mentions the custom in 1663), and servants of the wealthy were given the next day off to visit their families with a box of gifts, bonuses and sometimes leftover food. The origin is not definitive.",
    "angle": "Boxing Day was a thank-you, not a fight.",
    "claims": [
      {
        "id": "c1",
        "text": "In Britain, it was a custom for tradesmen to collect 'Christmas boxes' of money or presents on the first weekday after Christmas, as thanks for good service; the term dates back to the 17th century.",
